::SPEECH_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L05_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
A speaker says, "We should explain the claim before the examples so the audience understands our point." Which response best paraphrases the speaker?
{
~A. You think examples do not matter at all.#This changes the meaning. The speaker did not reject examples; the speaker suggested a better order.
=B. You are saying the claim should come first so the examples make more sense to the audience.#Correct. This keeps the speaker's meaning and restates it in new words.
~C. You want to skip the claim and use only examples.#This reverses the message. The speaker wants the claim explained first.
~D. You are saying the audience is not important.#This misses the main idea. The speaker specifically mentioned audience understanding.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L05_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
What is the main goal of paraphrasing during discussion?
{
=A. To restate the speaker's idea in your own words without changing the meaning#Correct. Paraphrasing checks understanding before the discussion moves forward.
~B. To add your opinion before understanding#This skips the listening step. A paraphrase should show understanding first.
~C. To make the speaker's idea sound completely different#This changes meaning. A paraphrase should preserve the meaning.
~D. To copy the speaker's exact words#Paraphrasing uses your own words. Copying exactly is repeating, not paraphrasing.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L05_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which response uses the best tone for respectful disagreement?
{
~A. Stop talking about that idea.#This shuts down discussion instead of responding respectfully.
~B. That idea is wrong, and mine is better.#This tone is dismissive. It attacks the idea without explaining a useful reason.
~C. I disagree because I do not like it.#This gives an opinion but not a communication reason.
=D. I understand your point, but I see it differently because the audience may need a more serious opening.#Correct. This response acknowledges the speaker, disagrees respectfully, and gives a reason.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L05_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
A classmate says, "The tone needs to be different." Which response best clarifies the message?
{
~A. That does not help at all.#This expresses frustration but does not ask for clarification.
=B. When you say different, do you mean more serious or more encouraging?#Correct. This asks a specific clarifying question and gives the speaker a clear way to explain.
~C. I will ignore that comment.#This avoids the chance to understand the speaker.
~D. You should know what different means.#This blames the speaker and does not clarify the idea.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L05_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which discussion response best builds on another person's idea?
{
~A. I was not listening to that idea.#This does not build on the idea because it shows no understanding.
~B. Your idea is not part of my section.#This dismisses the group connection instead of building the discussion.
=C. Your point about audience attention connects to our opening; we could add a short question there.#Correct. This connects to the speaker's idea and adds a useful next step.
~D. I do not want to discuss that point.#This stops discussion rather than developing the idea.
}
